(a) Appointment and qualifications. The county attorney shall be appointed by the chairman, by and with the advice and consent of the board of commissioners, and shall serve until a successor is appointed and has qualified. No person shall be so appointed unless he is a member in good standing of the State Bar of Georgia and has been actively engaged in the practice of law for at least three years preceding his appointment. The county attorney shall submit to the board of commissioners bills for his services, monthly or at such other reasonable periodic intervals as the parties may agree, containing descriptions or itemizations of the work performed during such periods.
(b) Powers and duties. The county attorney shall be the legal representative and advisor of the county. The county attorney shall upon the request of the board of commissioners, its chairman, or any other county officer or official:
(1) Provide his advice upon any legal matter;
(2) Draft, review, revise and comment upon contracts, ordinances, and other writings;
(3) Represent the county in judicial and administrative proceedings;
(4) Attend to any other legal matters affecting the county.
(Code 2008, § 3-501)

